// Vintage analysis: per (ticker, vintage_year, age_quarters) cumulative credit
// performance curves (dating engine restored 2026-09-25, with fixes).
//
// "Vintage" is assigned per loan by a dating waterfall (best evidence wins). The
// order is measured: hide a holder's own date, and those its adviser's other
// funds printed, and see which rule recovers it (output/vintage_legacy/holdout.json,
// data/vintage_golden.ts).
//   1. own_acq            — holder's own disclosed acquisition date (earliest
//                           ever printed); replaced when a peer holding the
//                           SAME tranche (same lien and maturity year, due within
//                           3 months) disclosed >90d earlier. A date printed as
//                           a merger or seed day is not a date
//                           (data/bdc_book_events.csv).
//   2. xbdc_borrow        — date of the same tranche at a fund of the same
//                           adviser (the deal's own date).
//   3. first_obs_reliable — the quarter this BDC first showed the loan, when its
//                           previous book is on file and no merger landed that
//                           quarter. first_obs_elsewhere when another holder showed
//                           the same tranche in an earlier year, unless a peer's
//                           date for the tranche (due within 3 months) falls in
//                           this BDC's year, when that sighting was of the loan it
//                           re-papered; holder_first for a piece of a borrower it
//                           already held (under any of its names), with a year's
//                           gap or little life left, unless it is new money (7+
//                           years left, or a return due later than anything it
//                           held). A date of the same tranche at another BDC
//                           (xbdc_borrow) in the year first sighting gives dates
//                           the day; one of another year gives way, and dates a
//                           loan with no sighting.
//   4. xbdc_near          — a peer's tranche maturing within 2 years.
//   5. ltail_enriched     — name-matched date from a long-tail fund's XBRL.
//   6. tenor_model        — maturity − calibrated tenor, never after first sighting.
//   7. xbdc_borrow_loose  — date from a DIFFERENT facility of the borrower.
// Sources 4-7, holder_first and merger_date are ESTIMATES, graded LOW. First
// sightings are MED with 5+ years left to maturity, else LOW. Disclosed and peer
// dates are graded by stability on the tranche (HIGH ≤90d drift/spread, MED
// ≤12mo, else LOW). A date more than two quarters AFTER our first sighting of the
// loan is rejected (next source wins); an own date replaced that way counts as a
// correction (MED). reborn_holder_first, defaulted paper (on non-accrual when first
// shown; or first shown the filing after its holder showed the borrower on
// non-accrual, or after it with a date printed from before it) dated from the
// holder's first loan to the borrower, is an estimate graded MED (undated when that
// loan came in a merger or an older BDC's first book). Beside another loan of its
// holder, borrower (under any of its names) and cohort whose default the curves
// count, it is out of the all-dated view, and out of the high-confidence view too
// unless that default is counted there by none but itself. Another holder's earlier
// sighting of the tranche caps every estimate. These are acquisition / first-seen
// dates, not proven facility origination dates.
//
// The curves count loans only. Equity, warrants and fund interests keep their
// dates in the exposure table but are out of every curve, and so are vehicles
// (CLO and other structured notes, JVs, funds, securitizations: scripts/58
// vehicle_rows), whose exits are neither repayments nor defaults. A loan is one
// holder's debt to one borrower in one instrument and maturity year, carried on
// through renames, relabels and extensions; debt papered for a new owner (its own
// date over a year after the earlier piece's, under another name or after a
// merger-day date) is a new loan, not an extension.
//
// Entry weight = the loan's cost in the first quarter we saw it, summed over
// all of its pieces. Every metric is weighted by entry cost.
//
// Right-censoring: at age k a cohort only counts loans whose vintage date is
// at least k quarters before their BDC's latest filing (n_loans_eligible,
// eligible_cost_b). Points with fewer than 20 eligible loans (30 for one BDC)
// or under 25% of the cohort's cost eligible are not published, so a curve
// simply stops. pct_eligible shows how much of the cohort is counted.
//
// Delayed entry: a loan is in a rate's base only from the age its holder first
// shows it (age 0 when that is the holder's first filing after the loan's date);
// pct_not_yet_seen is the eligible cost not shown yet. A loan first seen late
// already on non-accrual or below 80¢ is out of that series (the change could
